Aim: Critical Section Problem (Producer-Consumer) using Semaphores – Implement the Producer-Consumer problem using semaphores in C.

Theory: Producer-Consumer Problem
•	The Producer produces items and puts them into a shared buffer.
•	The Consumer takes items from the buffer.

What is a Semaphore?
A semaphore is a variable used in Operating Systems to control access to shared resources.

We use 3 semaphores:	
Empty:- Number of empty spaces
Full:- Number of available items
            Mutex:- Allows only one thread to access the buffer

Code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

int buffer[3];
int in = 0, out = 0;

sem_t empty;
sem_t full;
sem_t mutex;

// Producer
void *producer(void *arg)
{
    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
    {
        sem_wait(&empty);
        sem_wait(&mutex); //lock the buffer

        buffer[in] = i;
        printf("Produced: %d\n", i);
        in++;

        sem_post(&mutex); //unlock the buffer
        sem_post(&full); }

    return NULL;
}
// Consumer
void *consumer(void *arg)
{
    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
    {
        sem_wait(&full);
        sem_wait(&mutex);

        printf("Consumed: %d\n", buffer[out]);
        out++;

        sem_post(&mutex);
        sem_post(&empty);
    }
return NULL;
}

int main()
{
    pthread_t p, c;

    // Initialize semaphores
    sem_init(&empty, 0, 3);
    sem_init(&full, 0, 0);
    sem_init(&mutex, 0, 1);

    // Create threads
    pthread_create(&p, NULL, producer, NULL);
    pthread_create(&c, NULL, consumer, NULL);

    // Wait for threads
    pthread_join(p, NULL);
    pthread_join(c, NULL);

    return 0;
}

